ow much of a computation's granular history you choose to remember when you evaluate it. So if I run a terminal value collapse only cares about the final output of the code, if I ask a calculator for 2 plus 2, it just says 4, it doesn't care how the microchip got there. That's a coarse, low-level harmonic. It's smooth and broad. But a full provenance collapse tracks the entire history of every single microevaluation step the computer took to get to 4. That is a fine, high-level harmonic because it preserves all the complex, choppy detail of the journey. Exactly. The collapse operator fixes the indistinguishability relation over the space of computational histories. It is a mathematical dial that decides which details are erased as irrelevant and which are preserved as essential. Which leads fliction to a genuinely radical, almost aggressive claim about computer science itself. They state there is no such thing as two computations being really the same context independently. It is a profound axiom that challenges a lot of basic programming assumptions. You fundamentally cannot ask if two algorithms are the same without explicitly specifying your collapse operator. That makes total sense now. If you use a terminal value collapse, the low-resolution smooth wave, the algorithm 2 plus 2, and the algorithm 3 plus 1 are exactly the same computation they both output 4. But if you use a full provenance collapse, the high-resolution patchy wave, they are entirely geometrically different computations because the historical path they took to arrive at 4 is completely different. The equivalence of two ideas is entirely dependent on your chosen coordinate ontology. So abstract computer science forms a sphere. Physical fields and flatworms form a sphere. But fliction saves the ultimate plot twist for the very end of the book. What about human thought? What about observation statistics and the way our brains categorize reality? We enter Chapter 8, Distinguishability Geometry. And t